contrastLevel < 1.0) { return Lerp(medium, high, (contrastLevel - 0.5) / 0.5); } else { return high; } }};} // namespace material_color_utilities#endif // CPP_DYNAMICCOLOR_CONTRAST_CURVE_H_
=== /home/src/.config/quickshell/plugins/wallwatch/notes.md === [**MATUGEN**]1. INPUT SOURCE (wallpaper-image(path)/ color palette)2. MATUGEN-CORE-ENGINE3. GENERATE MATERIAL-YOU COLOR PALETTE4. APPLY CUSTOMIZATIONS (contrast, light/dark)5. PROCESS TEMPLATES6. OUTPUT (theme files, wallpaper outputs)* also has wallpaper integration- VARIANTS: primary, secondary, tertiary, error, neutral, neutral-variant (Each with 13)- uses auto restart once the wallpaper reloads- handles light/dark/amoled(true-black backgrounds) themesconfig.toml - input-path = contains the template path to the template used to create the right color variables for different configs (json, rasi, conf) - output-path= contains the file with the colors (where it's gonna end up) the output file will go with how the input-template-path is described**MATERIAL3DESIGN**- create harmonious palette colors- must have an expressive UI- DYNAMIC COLORS: color processing is automatic based on the wallpaper a single source color generates 5 key colors, which are used to make tonal palettes tones from the palettes are then assigned color roles, then those roles to the UI elements u only give it one color... (idk how ur gonna walk around ts)STEPS: 1. Get the source color through "quantization" 2. Feed the source color into an algorithm a) User-generated color algorithm - Source color from wall, Maps colors of specific tones (lighter or darker) into the scheme according to a combo of system design choices b) Content-based color algorithm - uses image for source color, tones adjust to match the appearace of the source image while maintaining accessible contrast c) Custom colors - colors closelt match the chosen input colors- the source color is generated into a full color-scheme in (a)- the source color creates a slightly different color scheme in (b) than in (a)- custom colors are made from each color (creates a scheme that is of that color [variants]) in (c) 3. the algorithm generates key colors [Primary, Secondary, Tertiary, Neutral, Neutral variant] - M3 manipulates the source color's HUE and CHROMAKey Color Hue Chroma Tone RangePrimary Same as source Same as source 40 (light) / 80 (dark)Secondary Same as source 50% of source chroma 40-45 (light) / 70-75 (dark)Tertiary Source hue Â± 60Â°Same as source 50 (light) / 60 (dark)Neutral Same as source 0 (grayscale) VariousNeutral Variant Same as source 10-20% of source chroma Various - Secondary fells like a muted primary; and Tertiary feels like a complementary color (opposite side of the color wheel) 4. the algorithm creates tonal palettes for each key color - colors in these palettes are given a number from 0-100 in increments of 10 then 90, 95, 98, 99, 100 - the smaller the tonal value the darker the color 5. The algorithm assigns tones to color roles - the algo uses accessible color relationships to assign specific tones to the 26 color roles in both light and dark theme - colors from the 5 tonal palettes are assigned to color roles eg: primary roles are picked from the primary tonal palette, while surface roles from the neutral tonal palette -dark theme colors are also automatically assigned. Apps receive now light and dark themes through a signle set of color roles 6. New colors are applied to the UI - the 26 standard color roles are already assigned to elements of the UI - when a new source color is picked, the UI dynamically changes colorCONSTRAST: LEVELS: Standard(default) - the baseline scheme already uses mixed levels of contrast (reduce cognitive load) Medium - minimum contrast ratio of 3:1 High - 7:1 contrast ratio to reduce visual distractions - they are applied for both light and dark themes - custom components can support contrast levels by using Material's appropriate color rolesPAIRING ACCESSIBLE TONES: - the system manipulates Hue,Chroma and Tone (HCT) to create a tonal palette for each color with tones from 0-100 (13) - tonal values range from 0(black) to 100(white) - material's color algorithms use these palettes to find and pair contrasting tones, creating accessible color combos - because tone can describe the lightness/darkness of a color, it can be used to assign a dark/darker tone on a buttons container color and a lighter tone on the text, ensuring a 3:1 contrast - for deeper contrasts, assigned tones are further apart eg 7:1DEFINING COLORS WITH HCT - defines all colors using 3D: hue, chroma, tone - hue and chroma can be manipulated without affecting tone - better than HSL and or RGB a) Hue - perception of a color (red, orange, yellow, green....) - is a number ranging from 0-360 - 0 and 360 have the same hue b)Chroma- how colorful or neutral(grey, black or white) the color appears - ranges from 0(completely grey, white or black) to infinity (most vibrant) but mostly cap at 120 - different hues and different tones have different maximum chroma values c)Tone - how light or dark a color appears (LUMINANCE) - range: 0(pure black) to 100 (pure white) - is crutial as it determines contrast - colors with greater difference in tone have higher contrasts, and vice versa Property HSL HCTPerceptually uniform? No â 50% lightness â  50% perceived brightness Yes â tone = perceived luminanceChroma independent of tone? No â changing lightness changes saturation Yes â hue/chroma can change without affecting toneAccessibility Hard to guarantee contrast Built-in contrast guarantees### COLOR-ROLES- the connective tissue btwn UI elements and what color goes where- color roles are mapped to Material components- color roles ensure accessibility: the color system is built on accessible color pairings- color roles are tokenized: Adesign token represents a small, reusable design decision that's part of a design system's visual style COLOR-ROLE-NAMING: Surface - role used for backgrounds and large, low emphasis areas of the screen Primary, Secondary, Tertiary - accent color roles used to emphasize or de-emphasize foreground elements Containder - roles used as a fill color for foreground elements (buttons). They should not be used for text or icons On - roles starting with On indicate a color for text/icons on top of its paired parent color eg {OnPrimary = text/icons in Primary fill color} Variant - roles ending with Variant offer low emphasis alternative to its non-variant pair eg {Outline Variant is a less emphasized version of Outline color}PAIRING AND LAYERING: - pair color roles well for legibility as the contrast lvls changeACCENT COLOR ROLES: Primary: for important actions and elements that need most emphasis primary -> high emphasis fills, text, and icons against surface On primary -> text and icons against primary Primary containder -> standout fill color against surface (for key concepts like FAB{Floating Action Buttons}) on primary container -> text and icons against primary container Secondary: elements that dont need immediate emphasis, like the selected state of navigation icon or a dismissive button secondary -> less prominent fille, text, and icons against surface on secondary -> text and iconst against secondary secondary container -> less prominent fill color against surface (for recessive components like tonal buttons) on secondary container -> text and icons against secondary container Tertiary: for smaller elements that need special emphasis but dont resuite immediate attention (badge or notification) tertiary -> complementary fills, text and icons against surface on tertiary -> text and iconst against tertiary tertiary container -> complementary container color against surface (components like input fields) on tertiary container -> text and icons against tertiary container Error: for error states (incorrect passwoed in a text field and so on...) error -> attention grabbing color against surface or fills, icons and text indicating urgency on error -> text and icons against error error container -> attention grabbing fill color against surface on error container -> text/icons in error container Surface: used for more neutral backgrounds, and container colors for components like cards, sheets, dialogs... surface -> default background color on surface -> text/icons against any surface or surface container color on surface variant -> lower-emphasis color for text/icons against any surface or surface container color *surface container roles based on emphasis* surface container lowest -> lowest emphasis color container (darkest in darkmode, lightest in light mode) surface container low surface container - default container color surface container high surface container highest -> high emphasis container color Inverse: applied selectively to components to achieve colors that are the revese of those in the surrounding UI (contrast effect) inverse surface -> background fills for elements which contrast against surface inverse on surface -> text and iconst against inverse surface inverse primary -> actionable elements (text buttons) against inverse surface Outline: outline -> important boundaries (text field outline) outline variant -> decorative elements (such as dividers) and when other elemnts provide 4.5:1 contrast 1. *[dont use outline] for components that contain multiple elements or for dividers(since they have different contrast requirements), use [outline variant]* 2. *[dont use outline variant] to create a visual hierarchy or define visual boundary of targets. Use [outline] or other colors providing 3:1 contrast with surface color*Add-on color Roles- these color roles are ignorable if unsure where to set themFixed accent colors a) [Primary fixed, secondary fixed, tertiary fixed] fill colors used against surface these colors maintain the same tone in light & dark themes whereas tone container colors which change btwn themes used in place of container roles where such fixed behavior is desired b) [Primary fixed dim, secondary fixed dim, tertiary fixed dim] provide a more emphasized tone of their respective counter part above may be used where a deeper color but same fixed behavior is needed *dont use where u expect the theme to change. They have contrast issues*On fixed accent colors a) on fixed used for any text and icons which sit on top of the corresponding Fixed color eg {on primary fixed = primary fixed} b) on fixed variant used for [text that needs lower emphasis] against the corresponding fixed color eg {on primary fixed variant = primary fixed}Bright & dim surface roles a) surface dim: dimmest surface color in light and dark themes b) surface bright: brightest surface color in light and dark themes- these keep their relative brightness across both light and dark theme- In surface: the mapped area is the brightest in light theme abd dimmest in dark theme (the color is inverted)- In Surface bright: the mapped area is the brightest in both light and dark (basically the opposive version in Surface (in dark theme))### COLOR SCHEMEa) Static. -brand consistent, predictable colors irregardless of the wallpaperb) Dynamic automatically create an accessible color scheme based on a specific source color u get: accessible colors personalized colors that change as per the user's wallpaper ability to use advanced customizations like chroma fidelity to alter the dynamic color output user-controlled contrast settingsGET THE SOURCE COLORa) User-generated color (from wallpaper) or a2) Multiple color sources(for specific aspects of the UI to vary in color(different)) - analize the wallpaper and a single color is selected from the source color, and tones are chosen and assigned to each color role - u only assign color roles (see Material Theme Builder)b) content based color (from in app content like music album/book cover) - the source image is analized through quantization, a single color selected as the source color and tones chosen and assigned to each color role <ADDITIONAL>[How source color is chosen]quantized colors -> scoring -> best source colorScoring algorithm: chroma: higher chroma = better (more vibrant) tone: penalized tones < 20(too dark) and > 80 (too bright) hue distribution: prefers colors that aren't isolated (color should represent a meaningful porion of the image) population: how many pixels the image share this color[Tonal palette structure]Tone Value Name Typical Use0 Black Pure black (AMOLED)10 Darkest Surface dim, dark mode backgrounds20 Very Dark Dark containers30 Dark Secondary backgrounds40 Medium-Dark Primary (light mode default)50 Medium Mid-range accents60 Medium-Light Tertiary (light mode)70 Light Hover states80 Very Light Primary (dark mode default)90 Lightest Container backgrounds (light mode)95 Near White Surface bright98 Almost White Surface (light mode default)99 Off-White Background (light mode)100 White Pure white *not all tones are used tor every color*[26 Color roles + add-ons]Category RolesPrimary primary, onPrimary, primaryContainer, onPrimaryContainerSecondary secondary, onSecondary, secondaryContainer, onSecondaryContainerTertiary tertiary, onTertiary, tertiaryContainer, onTertiaryContainerError error, onError, errorContainer, onErrorContainerSurface surface, onSurface, onSurfaceVariant, surfaceContainerLowest, surfaceContainerLow, surfaceContainer, surfaceContainerHigh, surfaceContainerHighestInverse inverseSurface, inverseOnSurface, inversePrimaryOutline outline, outlineVariantFixed primaryFixed, primaryFixedDim, secondaryFixed, secondaryFixedDim, tertiaryFixed, tertiaryFixedDim, onPrimaryFixed, onPrimaryFixedVariant, onSecondaryFixed, onSecondaryFixedVariant, onTertiaryFixed, onTertiaryFixedVariantBright/Dim surfaceDim, surfaceBrightWHAT UR DOING1. Implementing mathematical algorithms for: Color conversion RGB â XYZ â LAB â LCH â HCT functions Quantization Wu or Celebi algorithm to reduce image colors Scoring Math to pick the best source color Tone generation Creating 101 tonal variations of a color Scheme derivation Calculating secondary/tertiary from primary Role assignment Mapping tones to semantic names (primary, surface, etc.) - u whire the functions. You implement the math2. Following the specifications/ rules(Material3) primary = source color at tone 40(light) or 80(dark) secondary = same hue, half chroma tertiary = hue Â± 60Â°, same chroma contrast ratios >= 4.5:1 for text surface tones = 99(light)/ 10 (dark)- ur recreating its functionality in ur own code- ur reimplementing an existing, documented system- ur NOT copying google's code. Ur writing ur own implementation based on the PUBLIC SPECIFICATIONS- UR NOT LINKING TO THE C++ LIBRARYColor behavior When the wallpaper changes, colors update according to M3 rulesComponent styling Buttons, cards, dialogs look like Material DesignAccessibility Text contrast automatically meets WCAG standardsConsistency Primary, secondary, surface colors are derived correctlyTIPS:1. use test driven development2. keep a reference implementation [Material Color Utilities] {https://github.com/material-foundation/material-color-utilities} when ur code produces different results debug3. create a test harness (in the main file to print out the results)4. Get everything working first then profile and optimizePHASES:1. Color space foundationcolorspace.h/cpp RGB â XYZ â LAB conversions Every other function depends on thishct.h/cpp LAB â LCH â HCT, gamut mapping Core Material 3 color modeltest_colorspace.cpp Verify conversions round-trip Catch bugs early Zero external dependencies â just C++ and math Independent â doesn't need Qt, images, or anything else Testable â you can write unit tests immediately Foundation â everything else builds on this2. Tonal palettetonal_palette.h/cpp Generate 101 tones from source colortest_tonal_palette.cpp Verify tone 40 = source color, tone progression Depends only on Phase 1 (HCT conversions) Gives you the core Material 3 behavior You can now generate a full tonal range from any color3. Scheme generatorscheme_generator.h/cpp Generate primary, secondary, tertiary from sourcecolor_roles.h/cpp Map tones to semantic roles (surface, onPrimary, etc.)test_sch